    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic stress disorder, much improved after Cognitive Processing Therapy (F43.10)</content>. PCL-5 24, down 18 points from 42 and below the 31 to 33 range that suggests probable PTSD, which meets the goal of 27 or lower. He completed nine sessions, meeting the goal of eight. [S]<list><item>Keep using the therapy skills and worksheets.</item><item>Booster session with Dr. Lee if symptoms return.</item><item>No medication.</item><item>PCL-5 and PHQ-9 at a primary-care review in three months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Depressive symptoms, resolved</content>. PHQ-9 4, in the remission range of 4 or lower. [S]<list><item>PHQ-9 periodically in recovery, with the PCL-5.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic insomnia, improved (G47.00)</content>. Sleep onset about 20 minutes and six and a half to seven hours of sleep, with nightmares about once a week, without medication. [S]<list><item>Continue the sleep routine and fixed wake time.</item><item>Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia if the insomnia persists or returns.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Suicide risk review</content>. No suicidal ideation, intent, plan, or preparatory behavior since the last visit on the structured screen. Protective factors are unchanged, and acute risk remains low. [S]<list><item>Safety plan reviewed for life after therapy: warning signs, coping steps, and contacts kept, with Dr. Lee&apos;s clinic remaining a contact.</item><item>Lethal-means steps and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline (press 1) remain in place.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PCL-5</td><td>Baseline, then at set intervals during treatment (care plan: six and twelve weeks)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>24, down 18 points from 42; below the probable-PTSD range; goal of 27 or lower met</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline; at least monthly during active treatment; periodically in recovery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4, in the remission range; periodic checks in recovery</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Trauma-focused psychotherapy (Cognitive Processing Therapy)</td><td>Weekly course of about 12 sessions</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Course completed last week with nine sessions, meeting the goal of eight; booster sessions available</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS screener, since last visit)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative: no ideation, intent, plan, or behavior since the last visit</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Safety plan, lethal-means steps, and crisis resources</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed and updated for life after therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol and other substance use</td><td>At intake and during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep diary and agreed sleep routine</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Asleep in about 20 minutes; six and a half to seven hours</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia</td><td>If insomnia persists after PTSD treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Insomnia has improved alongside the PTSD; offered if it persists or returns</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Obstructive sleep apnea symptom screen</td><td>At the insomnia assessment; repeat for new symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Negative in August; no new symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>PTSD medication and sedative-hypnotics</td><td>If psychotherapy is declined or the response stalls</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Responded to psychotherapy alone; no medicines</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Mental status examination, vital signs, and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Euthymic with full-range affect; BP 126/80 mmHg; weight 73.9 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Laboratory tests</td><td>Only for medication monitoring or a suspected medical cause</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No medicines</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reduce PCL-5 score from 42 to 27 or lower by the twelve-week review. Outcome by 1964-09-30: PCL-5 score 24 [S]</item><item>Complete at least eight Cognitive Processing Therapy sessions by the twelve-week review. Outcome by 1964-09-30: Nine sessions completed [S]</item><item>Use the agreed sleep routine on at least five nights each week by the six-week review. Outcome by 1964-08-11: Patient-reported use on five to six nights each week [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder and Acute Stress Disorder (2023); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder and Obstructive Sleep Apnea (2025); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Adult preventive visit next month. PTSD and insomnia review with the PCL-5 and PHQ-9 in three months. Booster session with Dr. Lee as needed; the safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline (press 1) remain available at any time. Next scheduled visit 1964-10-27: adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

